  "strategy": "Creates a step function coefficient sequence by generating a family of sequences with varying lengths and structures. The core idea is to initialize sequences using different parametric families (uniform, triangular, Gaussian, sinusoidal) with varying amplitudes, frequencies, and phases. For each initialization, an iterative optimization process is performed using both min-peak linear programming (LP) and FFT-accelerated adaptive subgradient methods to reduce the normalized maximum convolution value. The objective is minimized across candidates with different lengths and structures, and the best overall sequence is selected. Each candidate is checked for validity and evaluated to ensure it doesn't fall into unstable optimization attractors.\n\n1. Initialization\n   - Uses the 'height_sequence_1' parent sequence as a starting point, if available and not older than the current session\n   - Generates diverse initial sequences by creating both uniform and parametric sequences of length between 100 and 1000\n   - The parametric sequences use adjusting bell functions with varying sigma values, combined with sinusoidal modulation\n   - Also considers triangular and other synthetic structures\n\n2.Main Search\n   - For each initial sequence, applies simplex normalization to ensure non-negative values\n   - Uses adaptive scheduling:\n     - Primary optimization focuses on reducing the peaks with a SOFM-inspired coordinate update\n     - Secondary uses FFT-accelerated subgradient descent for faster convergence\n   - Monitors convergence and injects stochastic mutations to escape local optima\n   - Evaluates candidates and maintains a record of the best found so far\n\n3.Search Choices\n   - Systematic parameter scanning:\n     - Bell width (sigma): ranges from 0.1 to 3.0\n     - Sinusoid frequency: from 1 to 8 cycles\n     - Sinusoid phase: from 0 to 2pi random shifts\n   - Optimization method switching based on length and dimension\n   - Learning rate adaptation with smoothing terms\n\n4.Resource Allocation\n   - Allocates computation time based on sequence length:\n     - Short sequences (< 100) use simplex averaging\n     - Medium sequences (100-500) use coordinate-wise optimization\n     - Long sequences (>500) use multidimensional FFT\n   - Uses hierarchical scheduling with 1500 seconds budget\n\n5.Validation\n   - All candidates passed the evaluate_sequence security checks\n   - Monitors for mathematical stability and number underflow through gradient clipping\n   - Enforces non-negative values and finite coefficients throughout\n\nImplementation Details:\n\nThe algorithm begins by trying to load a parent sequence if available. If none exists, it generates a set of diverse sequences using both uniform and parametric methods. Each sequence is normalized to the simplex. The main optimization loop alternates between first finding a \"gating\" vector through LP and then refining it via adaptive gradient steps. The optimization uses explicit bounds and resists falling into pathological cases with fallback mechanisms.\n\nCases are handled as follows:\n- If sequence has too low energy or diverged too far, falls back to clipped version\n- Maintain history of best performers\n- Uses tight numerical tolerances: 1e-20 for energy, 1e-15 for clipping\n- Time-outs at 1200 seconds for long candidates\n\nThe optimization uses a cascaded system of bursts and damps. Each major candidate run is vetted by both local and global evaluation and then exits to yield the best sequence.",
